ab and that's  all right thank you  no i'm still here oh sorry sorry are you  cut out i'm sorry  no it was me it was my computer just did  something terribly funny there um  apologies but no you don't need to limit  of the the number of levels that are  placed on the slide i think what you  would need to do though is be really  careful  about microtumia and kind of floating  stage when you're putting your samples  on the slide to make sure that they are  not only inside the boundaries of your  coverslip but that they're also inside  the the boundaries of the scan region  for the scanner that you would be using  and as much as possible bring them close  together which and  kind of touched on is going to give you  a smaller scan area  and a smaller file size and faster  throughput overall  for your your scanners so  i wouldn't  i wouldn't recommend necessarily  changing away from some of the practices  on how tissue is presented  um but just are there steps that can be  done to optimize  um those processes  next question  can the system read slides i mean after  scanning the slide an h e stain slide  will the ai tell if the tissue is normal  or pathologic  um  again great question and this is really  where  we see an awful lot of research and  development and innovation around this  space and both with h e slides  um and then across a range of stains and  both for for clinical or research  applications  um  there's no  kind of silver bullet or magic app out  there  that is doing this and for some of the  products that have been released to  market already they're done on a  kind of subspecialty or tissue based  um  system at the moment as opposed to  you know any tissue on any slide in any  lab being put through it and saying you  know is it normal or is it a tumor um  but it's a  massively accelerating area at the  moment with so much focus and interest  um  there's a lot of indications that this  is the direction  that the technology is moving  great thank you  our 