nce  and so detect a piece on the the slide  where there is no tissue or no sample  and  and look to to white balance on that so  effectively normalize on your your color  profile and in that way i think if the  question was more in relation to the the  consistency  of staining between slides  and i think with that then is  where possible avoid manual staining  where you're doing manual dilutions of  your stains  and look at your you're kind of ready to  use  um  type reagents where possible and  automation  and i think the more we can look at  bringing in automation into our  processes the more we can drive  standardization  and remove any kind of manual errors  that  are just inherently prone to manual  processes  all right thank you  next question  if there is a small artifact above the  tissue after cover slipping  does all of the tissue on the  digital image appear blurry or does this  just cause a few areas of blurriness  around the artifact does the scanner see  the artifact in the only scanned focal  plane or does it take more than one  focal plane to complete the slide scan  that is a great question  um  and again it's one that doesn't  necessarily have a straight yes or no  answer  and  so this is really going to be dependent  on the scanner and the scanner mechanism  so  some scanners will employ a technique  where they lay down a focus map  first so they will automatically detect  um  where your your tissue is and then they  will lay focus points on top of that  tissue to  kind of map that micro topography um  across the the tissue and be able to  then have a scan map that they it  follows so if one of those scan points  happens to be on a piece of debris or  artifact yes it can throw out the scan  map  and that could be the amount of  blurriness in that image would be  dependent on the size of the artifact  and kind of where that that next focal  point um or focus point  is within the the tissue  and other scanners will do a type of  real-time focusing 